{"tool_name":"geneselector","contents":["oeb","bioschemas","biotools"],"fetched_metadata":{"biotools":{"id":"geneselector","home":"http://bioconductor.org/packages/release/bioc/html/GeneSelector.html","license":"GPL-2.0","summary":"Filter selecting those genes which are consistently identified as differentially expressed using various statistical procedures. 'Selected' genes are those present at the top of the list. The stability of the findings can be considered in the final ranking by examining perturbed versions of the original data set, e.g. by leaving samples, swapping class labels, generating bootstrap replicates or adding noise. Given multiple ranked lists, one can use aggregation methods to find a synthesis.","addition_date":"2017-01-17T15:02:13Z","last_update_date":"2018-12-10T12:58:54Z","tool_type":["Command-line tool","Library"],"version":["2.24.0"],"collections":["BioConductor"],"biotools_curie":"biotools:geneselector","credit":[{"email":"ms@cs.uni-sb.de","name":"Martin Slawski","typeEntity":"Person","typeRole":["Primary contact"]}],"documentation":[{"type":["User manual"],"url":"http://bioconductor.org/packages/release/bioc/html/GeneSelector.html"}],"edit_permission":{"type":"private"},"function":[{"operation":[{"term":"Filtering","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_3695"},{"term":"Gene expression analysis","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_2495"}]}],"name":"GeneSelector","operating_system":["Linux","Mac","Windows"],"owner":"bioconductor_import","publication":[{"doi":"10.1093/bib/bbp034","metadata":{"abstract":"Ranked gene lists are highly instable in the sense that similar measures of differential gene expression may yield very different rankings, and that a small change of the data set usually affects the obtained gene list considerably. Stability issues have long been under-considered in the literature, but they have grown to a hot topic in the last few years, perhaps as a consequence of the increasing skepticism on the reproducibility and clinical applicability of molecular research findings. In this article, we review existing approaches for the assessment of stability of ranked gene lists and the related problem of aggregation, give some practical recommendations, and warn against potential misuse of these methods. This overview is illustrated through an application to a recent leukemia data set using the freely available Bioconductor package GeneSelector. \u00a9 The Author 2009. Published by Oxford University Press.","authors":[{"name":"Boulesteix A.-L."},{"name":"Slawski M."}],"citationCount":125,"date":"2009-08-31T00:00:00Z","journal":"Briefings in Bioinformatics","title":"Stability and aggregation of ranked gene lists"},"type":["Primary"]}],"topic":[{"term":"Gene expression","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_0203"},{"term":"Genetics","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_3053"}],"validated":1},"bioschemas":{"name":"GeneSelector","home":"https://bio.tools/geneselector","license":"GPL-2.0","version":"2.24.0","summary":"Filter selecting those genes which are consistently identified as differentially expressed using various statistical procedures. 'Selected' genes are those present at the top of the list. The stability of the findings can be considered in the final ranking by examining perturbed versions of the original data set, e.g. by leaving samples, swapping class labels, generating bootstrap replicates or adding noise. Given multiple ranked lists, one can use aggregation methods to find a synthesis.","tool_type":"sc:SoftwareApplication"},"oeb":{"metrics":[{"@id":"https://openebench.bsc.es/monitor/metrics/biotools:geneselector:2.24.0/cmd/bioconductor.org","@license":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/","@nmsp":"biotools","@type":"metrics","deprecated":true,"distribution":{"sourcecode":{"interpreted":true}},"project":{"build":{},"deployment":{},"documentation":{"manual":true},"license":{"open_source":true,"osi":true},"publications":[{"entries":[{"cit_count":53,"citations":[{"count